Python only: Supply Chain now reports every package declared in a `requirements.txt`, not only the ones pinned with `==`. A requirement with a version range (`requests>=2.0`, `numpy~=1.26`, `django==2.2.*`, `requests (>=2.0)`) is reported with the declared specifier as its version, and a bare name (`flask`) is reported with an empty version, so they appear in the dependency inventory and SBOM instead of being dropped with a "not pinned to an exact version" parse error. These dependencies have no definitive installed version, so for now they produce no Supply Chain findings. When there is no `requirements.in` manifest to consult, a range or bare-name line is reported as a direct dependency, since it was declared by hand rather than written by a resolver; exact pins keep their previous unknown transitivity. Local path and URL lines are no longer reported as parse errors, while a line whose leading token is not a valid package name (`requests$foo>=1`) is now reported as a parse error instead of being dropped silently. Other ecosystems and other Python manifest formats are unchanged.
